course: dictionary
lesson: 14084
title: Helping people agree
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When two people disagree, sometimes they need a fair helper to get them talking again. The helper's job is to mediate. «Mrs Patel mediated between Ravi and Lena, so they could agree on who would go first.» The clues are between, agree and disagree, so mediate must mean help two sides talk and reach an agreement.
explain: A mediator does not pick a winner. «Ravi did not decide who was right; he let each girl explain her side.» That is the key idea: a mediator stays neutral, which means not taking sides. Always ask: does my meaning fit the sentence? Try putting «helped them agree» in place of mediated and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Mediate can also have a more formal meaning: to be the means by which something passes between two things. This is less common in everyday talk, so read the words around it for clues.
fact: Mediate is a verb: I mediate, she mediates, they mediated, we are mediating.
fact: A person who mediates is called a mediator.
fact: «Mediate» means to help two people or groups who disagree to talk and find an agreement, without taking sides.
fact: At school, «peer mediators» are pupils trained to help classmates settle playground arguments.
fact: In formal use, «mediate» means to be the means by which something passes or happens between two things, as in «Christians believe Jesus mediates between God and people».
exercise: «The teacher mediated the argument so both boys could agree.» What does mediated mean? | Won the argument for one boy | Shouted at both boys until they stopped | *Helped them talk and agree, without taking sides | Ran away from the problem
exercise: Which person is a mediator? | A pupil who tells the teacher nothing at all | A pupil who starts every fight | *A pupil who helps two classmates settle an argument fairly | A pupil who always picks the winner
exercise: «The two countries asked a neutral leader to mediate.» Why must a good mediator stay neutral? | So they can leave the room early | So they never have to speak | So they can win a prize | *So both sides feel they are treated fairly
test: «Christians believe Jesus mediates between God and people.» What does mediates mean here? | Argues with | Chases after | *Acts as the link between | Forgets about
test: Fill the gap: «Yesterday the coach ____ the dispute between the two players.» | mediates | mediate | *mediated | mediator
exam: Ella asks, «Is a mediator a judge who decides who wins?» What is the answer? | *No, a mediator helps two sides agree and does not take sides | Yes, a mediator is the same as a referee who gives points | Yes, a mediator always decides the winner | No, a mediator only watches and never speaks
